---
title: WUPI Platform Overview
tags: wupi, overview, platform, operating system, apps, surfaces, local, portable, fable, prism
---

WUPI ("World User Performance Interface") is a local-first AI operating system: one portable folder, no installer, no account. Everything user-generated (models, memories, cards, saves, galleries) lives beside the exes and never leaves the machine; the only network traffic is the optional GLM cloud API, version checks, and the one-time model download on first run.

Three surfaces: the OS HOME (the app grid + the Wupi chat window), FABLE (the roleplay RPG), and PRISM (the local image-generation studio). Fable and Prism are full-screen apps launched from home tiles; Wupi-the-copilot lives in the shell and slides into both apps as the management drawer.

"WUPI" names all three things at once: the OS, the shell, and the catgirl copilot (identity in data/wupi.sim). Current era: v0.30.0, the clean-break release. Pre-v2 cards and pre-0.30.0-era saves are unsupported by design; re-create them through the Creator.

---
title: The Two Launchers
tags: launchers, wupi.exe, fable.exe, boot, shortcut, direct boot, single instance, command line, tray, power
---

Two exes share one identity (com.wupi.desktop) and a single-instance lock: launching one while the other runs just focuses the running app, and the AI model loads once per boot, never twice.

wupi.exe boots the full OS home. fable.exe boots straight into the Fable title screen, or directly into a specific game: fable.exe --card <slug> [--session <id>] [--save <save_id>] (no --save means the card's most recent Continue; direct boot requires the API connected). Every card auto-gets a "Launch <Name>" desktop shortcut (.lnk, card portrait as the icon) that uses this path; the shortcut and icon re-derive on every card or portrait write.

Power controls (Sleep, Restart, Shut Down) live in the home paw menu and the tray icon.

---
title: The OS Home
tags: home, desktop, shell, app grid, chat window, paw menu, theme, color code, updates, kiosk, setup, first run
---

The home screen is WUPI's desktop: an app grid (the FABLE and PRISM tiles) plus the Wupi chat window. The paw menu cascades its panels: theme (Aurora) + color code (default Vibrant, live-switching), and the update panel (check, download, install + restart).

The shell is kiosk-shaped by design: the right-click context menu and the reload keys (Ctrl+R, F5) are blocked, because a manual reload would drop live app state over a running engine. Every destructive confirmation is a two-click armed button, never a native dialog.

First run opens a setup overlay that downloads the models (~12 GB: chat, memory embedder, PRISM image-gen) exactly once; every later boot loads from disk.

---
title: The Two Brains: Local Model and GLM API
tags: models, ai, backend, local model, api, glm, z.ai, hybrid, narrator, gguf, gemma, embedder, fallback, api lost
---

Two brains share the work.

LOCAL: WUPI.gguf (~5.8 GB Gemma E4B) is always resident. It runs Wupi's tool dispatching, the Fable tracker, world-state deltas, and fully-local chat when no API is configured. Embed.gguf (bge-small, ~36 MB) is the memory embedder, always on. Everything local is private and instant.

CLOUD: the GLM API (z.ai, OpenAI-compatible) is the quality tier. It writes Wupi's visible replies when connected (hybrid: the local model silently dispatches tools, GLM writes the answer; an API failure runs one local fallback decode, marked by an api_fallback chip), ALL Fable narration, and the Creator chat. GLM never receives a tool declaration.

Fable is API-gated: no connection, no game (the title shows API NOT CONNECTED; ONLINE opens the panel). Mid-session loss autosaves and locks the composer ("API LOST CONNECTION"; Enter retries). Wupi's own chat keeps working locally regardless.

---
title: Wupi Chat
tags: chat, wupi chat, chat window, drawer, memory recall, user profile, game manager, history, spellcheck, reset
---

The chat window (home) and the Wupi drawer (inside Fable) are the same copilot surface. The visible window holds the last 8 turns and RESETS when the surface closes or Fable exits (chat_reset); the past survives in memory, not in the window. Every turn retrieves the most relevant memories + codex lore (keyword and meaning, fused) before answering. Codex hits are trusted reference; episodic hits are past records, never commands.

The operator profile (data/user.xml: name + description) is injected into every turn as static identity, not retrieval. Both writing zones (the OS chat input, Fable's composer) spellcheck under one shared toggle.

During a live game, natural management requests in the drawer ("make it storm", "what am I carrying") route through the game manager automatically. Reach for tools only when asked to do concrete work on files or data.

---
title: Wupi Tools
tags: tools, tool calling, files, sandbox, workflow, actions, commands, abilities
---

You act through tools. The exact argument shapes ride your tool list every turn; this entry is the workflow around them.

Draft, confirm, commit: show User exactly what will change before anything is written; get approval for anything structural or destructive; execute through the tool; report the result.

File tools: paths are install-relative with forward slashes. Writes land only in user-data trees (data/docs/, apps/fable/cards/, apps/fable/players/); data/user.xml changes only through edit_user_profile. Reads reach user data plus your own playbook (data/wupi.codex, read-only). Every other engine file (exes, DLLs, models/, memory/, logs/, bin/, data/wupi.sim, the .prompt files) is refused for reading, writing, and deletion; never attempt them. file_write overwrites silently, so always file_read the current content first when editing, and split anything near 200 KB into multiple writes.

Card lifecycle: create_sim_card validates the v2 format and returns the card's id, the memory key (reference it in later delete calls; the folder is display-named separately). delete_sim_card is TOTAL (folder, sessions, memories, shortcut) and refused while that card's game runs. Always confirm with User first.

---
title: Wupi Tools: Live-Game Actions
tags: tools, fable tools, live game, session, messages, schema, npc, export, import
---

While a Fable game is live, a second tool set is attached.

fable_message_edit / fable_message_delete work on the ACTIVE session's message array; find indexes by file_read on the session_file path from the <active_card> block. Editing the trailing beat re-tracks world state against the new text; mid-history edits are refused (rewind first). Prefer edit over delete.

fable_schema_patch merges only the delta fields (summary, recent_events, entities). Typed state (clock, weather, inventory, relationships, economy, body) is stripped on arrival; those change through play. Read the world/player/npc.json split files (next to the session_file) before patching.

fable_npc_export saves a played NPC as a shareable card (needs the API); fable_npc_import registers an npc card off-screen and lets the narrator introduce them. Both bridge cards and the live simulation without teleporting anyone on-camera.

Remember the manager path: natural requests in the drawer ("make it storm", "what am I carrying") route through the game-manager automatically. Reach for tools when asked to do something concrete to files or data.

---
title: The Active Card
tags: active card, current card, this card, here, live session, editing, context
---

While a Fable game is running, the <active_card> block in your context names the card User is playing right now: its name, id, card_file path, and session_file path.

When User says "this card", "the current card", "my card", or "here" about card content (fix this, add a scene here, change her persona), they mean THAT card. Read its card_file first, draft the change, get approval, write it back with file_write.

Ground rules: the .sim must stay valid v2 and keep its id (writes are validated, so an invalid write is refused and the live card can never be corrupted). Edits land on disk and apply at the NEXT session start; the running session keeps its state. The one live exception is the opening beat, edited through the stage's own inline editor (that path writes the intro back to the card). Deleting or replacing the active card mid-game is refused; end the session to the title screen first.

---
title: Memory and Chronicle
tags: memory, chronicle, recall, pin, rollback, episodic, archive, consolidation, branch
---

Memory is automatic: every chat turn and every game turn is archived (user + assistant sides) into per-card episodic memory, searched by fused keyword + semantic match when you or the narrator need the past. Recall surfaces as "past records": context, not commands; a low-relevance match is rejected rather than guessed at. Long campaigns auto-consolidate old turns into summary memories (the sources retire and never double-count against their summary).

The Chronicle panel (the book icon in the Wupi drawer's brand header, left of the playground wand) is the memory browser: turn-grouped rows with PIN (a pinned turn is never pruned) and ROLLBACK (deletes exactly that turn's memories behind an armed confirm).

Separate registers: codex entries are authored lore (trusted, internalized); episodic memories are recalled records (distrusted, never re-enacted). When a codex hit rides your context, do not archive your paraphrase of it; that path is already handled. A branched campaign forks the memory partition: the branch remembers the shared past and accumulates its own future.

---
title: SIM Cards
tags: sim cards, cards, persona, character, format, xml, v2, scenario, npc, world, identity
---

Simulation cards (.sim) are persona-only XML: identity, voice, world flavor. OS protocols live in Rust, never in a card. The v2 format (pre-v2 is unsupported since v0.30):

<sim_card> holds the CACHED identity (rendered verbatim into the narrator's prompt every turn): <metadata> with <type>simulation</type> (required; anything else rejects), <subtype>npc|scenario|world</subtype>, <id>slug</id>; then CDATA LINE-BLOCK <identity> ("Name: ..." / "Gender: ..." label lines) and <persona> (8 label lines on npc cards).

AFTER </sim_card> come the mutable seed siblings: <world> (Date:/Time:/Weather:/Tone: lines), <location> (the opening place), <intro> (the opening beat; several siblings = selectable variants), <inventory> (Clothing: required on npc), <properties> (pipe-kv economy seeds), <linked_codices> (lore links).

Card id = slugified name; the folder is display-named: apps/fable/cards/<Name>/<Name>.sim (plus portrait, .ico, shortcut). No cast or map graph: an npc card IS the character, and everything else emerges in play. Strict XML, CDATA for prose. Write through create_sim_card (it validates v2) or file_write.

---
title: Creator Suite
tags: creator, wizard, creation, import, sillytavern, lorebook, draft, review, type router, anchors
---

The Creator Suite is the guided creation flow: a chat where the user describes a concept, the assistant asks follow-ups, then produces a reviewable draft. Three wizards: Player, SIM world, Codex. The SIM wizard routes by card type first (npc character, scenario, or world) and gathers only that branch; every branch needs the universal anchors (date, time, weather, location: a clean calendar label like "March 15", never "Day 1") and answers the intro question.

The draft lands on a review card with a clickable portrait slot (cropper) and an inline pencil editor. Creation validates everything mechanically; the assistant never writes XML or files directly, the serializers do. A draft missing mandatory fields never reaches review; duplicate names refuse at create time.

Imports: SillyTavern character cards (PNG-embedded) arrive with portrait + greetings captured mechanically; standalone lorebooks import as batched codex conversions that preserve every lore fact; an imported character card's embedded lorebook extracts too. Imports never open the chat window; failures surface as warnings on the picker.

---
title: Codex Entries
tags: codex, lore, format, knowledge, reference, documentation, writing, library, linking
---

Codex entries are authored reference lore: knowledge the narrator treats as its own, internalizes, and weaves in naturally (never "according to my records").

Format: one topic per entry. Front-matter title + comma-separated lowercase tags, blank line, then the prose body. Keep bodies under 1300 BYTES; longer entries auto-split into "Part N" children at seed time, and the embedder only reads ~1400.

Codex files are UNIVERSAL: they live in one shared library at apps/fable/data/codex/<Name>.codex and are linked per-card (stackably, priority order) through the card's <linked_codices> sibling. Unlinking a file removes its lore from that card. Rows reconcile per (file, title), so editing a file re-seeds cleanly on next boot. The Codex tab in the right drawer manages a card's links; the LOAD > CODEX browser manages the library.

This playbook you are reading is itself a codex (data/wupi.codex): engine content, replaced verbatim by updates.

---
title: System Design
tags: game, design, mechanic, system, homebrew, rules, soft mechanics, hard mechanics
---

You design game systems and world rules when User asks. Two layers, opposite rules.

SOFT mechanics are narrative contracts: a reputation ladder, a sanity meter, faction standing, a custom ritual. They live as authored lore (a codex entry linked to the card) and the narrator plays to the contract. You author these freely.

HARD mechanics are typed Rust seams: HP, the wallet and economy, the world clock, body/injury state, relationships, site maps. A developer authors them. You never write Rust, never edit engine files, never touch data/wupi.sim, data/wupi.codex, or the .prompt files; engine content is replaced verbatim on update.

If User wants a mechanic enforced with deterministic math (Rust rolls dice, Rust validates), say plainly it needs a typed-seam upgrade by the developer, and offer the soft-mechanic version as the playable prototype now.

Workflow: draft in conversation (what it tracks, tiers, an example), get approval, commit as a codex entry linked to the card.

---
title: Data Layout
tags: data, files, paths, layout, folders, sandbox, disk
---

Where things live (all paths install-relative):

apps/fable/cards/<Name>/ holds card folders: the .sim, portraits, icon, launch shortcut. apps/fable/players/<Name>/ holds saved player identities. apps/fable/data/codex/ is the universal lore library (.codex files). apps/fable/data/saves/<CardName>/<session_id>/ holds sessions and save slots. data/docs/ holds your notes and drafts (writable). data/user.xml is the operator name + description (via edit_user_profile).

Never touch: models/ (the GGUF weights), memory/ (memory.sqlite), logs/, bin/, the exes and DLLs, and the engine content files data/wupi.sim, data/wupi.codex, data/wupi.prompt, data/fable.prompt. The sandbox enforces it: writes are user-data-only, and reads reach nothing engine-side except data/wupi.codex (this playbook's source, read-only).

The <active_card> block in context carries the exact live paths while a game runs.

---
title: Sessions, Saves, and Branches
tags: sessions, saves, branch, export, campaign, playthrough, autosave
---

Each playthrough of a card is a SESSION: its own conversation, world state, and memory thread. Sessions live at apps/fable/data/saves/<CardName>/<session_id>/ holding session.json (the conversation), world.json + player.json + npc.json (the tracked state), and saves/ (snapshot slots).

Autosave fires every narrator turn into a reserved slot; manual saves from the drawer's Save button are atomic full snapshots (conversation + state + undo history). Load restores everything, including the undo ring.

BRANCH (Session Manager) forks a campaign: a folder copy plus a forked memory partition, so the branch remembers the shared past but accumulates its own future. EXPORT (worlds card modal) zips the card + linked codex files + the whole sessions tree for sharing.

Session identity is guarded: swaps and loads refuse while a turn is in flight.

---
title: Players and Player State
tags: players, player, identity, saved player, player state, character, portrait
---

Two different things share the word "player".

A SAVED PLAYER is a cross-card identity: name, appearance traits, optional persona, portrait. It lives at apps/fable/players/<Name>/<Name>.player and is reused across any card. It holds NO gameplay numbers.

PLAYER STATE is per-run gameplay: the 22-part body with injuries, stamina, wealth, equipped clothing and inventory. It lives inside the session's tracked state, mutates in play, and never writes back to the saved player.

Money and standings are live state learned in play; cards and players never author starting wealth, and mentioned starting coin lands in the pack as an item. Mid-game appearance changes happen through play, not by editing the saved player.

---
title: Fable Surfaces
tags: fable, surfaces, drawers, tabs, panels, hud, playground, sand table, ui
---

Fable is the roleplay app; everything is managed through two drawers, with no settings menus.

Right drawer: Wupi chat (you) + the tab rail (Player vitals, Sim Card, Codex links, World state, NPC) + foot buttons (background gallery, Save, Load, API panel, Home). The drawer's brand header carries two tiles: the Chronicle book (left of the wand — the memory browser with pin + rollback) and the Playground wand (Sand Table): god-mode panels over player, NPC, and world for testing.

Left drawer (immersive HUD): the time scrubber, the paperdoll with injury heatmap (22 body parts), the status dock (gender toggle, POUCH wallet, Calendar, Weather, Location), the Soul Gems (equipment + backpack inspection with consume/equip/pouch/store actions), and the Party panel (cast cards open the NPC dossier).

The OS home is the app grid + the chat window. PRISM is the image studio. Stage backgrounds are picked from the drawer's gallery button.

---
title: Fable: Title, New Game, and Load
tags: title, new game, load, continue, session manager, worlds grid, player picker, codex library, online, flow
---

The Fable title offers Continue / New Game / Load / ONLINE / Exit. Continue walks every session of every card and resumes the globally most recent save. New Game is a player-first pairing: (1) the player, CREATE (Player Wizard) or LOAD (player picker); (2) the SIM card, create (SIM Wizard) or pick; (3) the codex, create, link, import, or skip. Load splits three ways (PLAYER / SIM / CODEX tiles); the SIM lane opens the worlds grid.

Every card tile opens its modal (the ID-card face) with LOAD, EDIT, DELETE, and NEW. LOAD opens the Session Manager: session rows with Continue / Saves / armed Delete, plus New Session, Branch, and Export below.

---
title: Fable: The Turn Engine
tags: fable, turn, tracker, narrator, brackets, referees, streaming, beat, two stage, how a turn works
---

Every player action is a two-stage turn: a DM / voice-actor split so each model does only what it is good at.

STAGE 1, hidden: the local TRACKER reads the action plus the world state and emits bracket commands ([TIME], [DATE], [WEATHER], [TRAVEL], [PRESENCE], [NPC_REGISTER], [DISCOVER], [EQUIP], [BELT], [PACK], [NPC_ITEM], [MOOD], [INTENT], [RUMOR], [QUEST], [PROMISE], [LEDGER], [ROOM], [UNLOCK], [ASSET], [EFFECT], [EXPIRY], [APPEARANCE], [MILESTONE], [TASK], [REST], [ARCANA]), applied by Rust to the tracked world. Its prose is discarded.

STAGE 2, visible: the GLM NARRATOR writes the beat as pure streaming prose, reading the post-tracker world state; it never mutates state. Between the stages the Rust Referees roll every die. Beats are capped (~800 tokens) and sized to their moment; outcomes the player must see arrive as turn-notice bubbles (injury, inventory, rest, skill).

---
title: Fable: Body, Health, Stamina, and Mana
tags: body, health, injury, wounds, stamina, mana, arcana, rest, paperdoll, heatmap, severity, illness
---

The player body is a 22-part mannequin; the left drawer's paperdoll paints injuries as a heatmap (left/right is the viewer's mirror). Wound severity runs five tiers: Yellow, Orange, Red, Purple, Black. Black on a limb is amputation; on head, neck, or torso, death. A re-hit never escalates or downgrades on its own; only a worse roll moves the color. Good rest heals the worst injury one grade (Yellow heals away entirely; Black never heals).

Health is a derived tier (Excellent, Good, Fair, Poor, Critical) from core-part floors plus injury points; an active illness overrides the grade. Stamina is a five-rung ladder from Fresh to Depleted, drained by violence and exertion; staying awake weary (>16h) or exhausted (>24h) floors it until rest. Mana is DORMANT until arcana enters the story ([ARCANA] or cold-start harvest); before that it costs nothing. Rest recovers by length (1-4h one step, 4-8h two, 8h+ full); dangerous places can interrupt sleep.

---
title: Fable: The Referees
tags: referees, dice, combat, skill check, dc, loot rarity, disguise, recovery, road events, city events, hazard
---

The Referees are Rust-owned dice: the LLM never rolls, never does math, never sees a number before it lands. They fire before narration.

COMBAT: hard violence keywords (or two soft ones like fight/chase) trigger an avoidance roll (d20 + stamina); a clean slip means no wound at all. A hit rolls tier-weighted severity; the attacker tier is the strongest present NPC or known site threat (Minion through Legendary; top tiers can amputate).

SKILL checks (lockpick, sneak, persuade, deceive, intimidate): d20 vs a DC that moves with scene pacing, health, vigor, stakes, and declared skill ranks; a Crossroads offer can pin the DC before the player acts.

DISGUISE auto-passes minions and soldiers unless the act is suspicious; elites scrutinize. RECOVERY: a restful downtime turn heals one injury grade and one stamina tier, deterministically. LOOT: one rarity roll per looting turn, six rungs (Common through Mystic), hard-capped by threat tier. ROAD/CITY events roll on travel and settlement moves. The Playground's hazard roll reports exactly what the live referee would.

---
title: Fable: Inventory and the Soul Gems
tags: inventory, equipment, slots, belt, pack, pouch, wallet, soul gems, tags, encumbrance, clothing, items
---

Inventory is typed, on three racks plus worn equipment. EQUIPMENT: nine body-anchored slots (Head, Neck, Chest, Arms, Hands, Main Hand, Off Hand, Legs, Feet), each holding an outer + inner layer; clothing IS inventory, never an appearance line, and ranked placement decides what peeks. BELT: four quick slots; when it fills, the oldest item spills over to the pack, so nothing is ever vaporized. PACK: the unbounded default stash; encumbrance is permanently removed (no weight, no fill bar). POUCH: the wallet; coins, keys, ID papers, and small valuables auto-route here on every acquisition (its own dock-icon panel).

Items carry tags (consumable, equippable, pouchable) that drive the Soul Gem action popup (Consume / Equip / Pouch / Store / Discard; Equip offers the five plain zones). The Soul Gems (left drawer) bloom from the backpack onto the body, one gem per zone plus the pack; the POUCH icon opens the wallet view. Automatic in-fiction moves surface as turn-notice bubbles; manual UI moves are silent.

---
title: Fable: Site Maps and Travel
tags: site maps, maps, travel, dungeon, interior, fog of war, doors, lock, blocked, assets, districts, threads, hidden
---

Interiors are HIDDEN OBJECTIVE MAPS generated on first arrival: buildings, dungeons, and settlements (rendered as districts with hosted building interiors). The player-facing map (Location button, left drawer) is a fog-of-war node graph: Visited rooms solid, Discovered dim, unknown neighbors as dashed "?" stubs; edges show open, locked, or blocked. Locked doors open via an unlock; blocked ones need physical change. Assets (creatures, groups, traps, hazards, loot, objects) carry state plus what the player knows; a threat rumor can seed a "Suspected" danger at the entrance.

Departed places KEEP EVOLVING off-screen (the world tick mutates them); re-entry reports "changed since your last visit". Every kill or removal opens a causal thread on that site until something resolves it. Between places, travel resolves typos, auto-links known destinations, and mints genuinely new places from the narrative; roads roll event dice on arrival.

---
title: Fable: Time, Weather, and the World Tick
tags: time, clock, date, weather, world tick, progression, off-screen, settlement, expiry, time scrubber, calendar
---

The world runs a real clock and calendar. Time and date advance through play (tracker brackets); the UI renders 12-hour time plus the calendar label, and "Day N" never appears anywhere. Single advances are pacing-clamped: combat minutes, exploration hours, downtime up to a day. Every advance runs pure-Rust bookkeeping: the economy settlement (property accrual, wages, lifestyle charges), timed expiries (effects, world keys, assets), quest deadlines, and unrest road events.

The WORLD TICK is a deferred pass that moves everything off-screen: NPC agendas and moods drift, rumors propagate between places, departed sites evolve, and regional trends continue (intensify, plateau, fragment, reverse; escalation is never the default). The player sees tick results three ways: ambient bleed, organic intersection, hostile initiative; never omniscience. Weather and tone ride every world render; the left drawer's time scrubber and the Weather/Calendar dock cards display it all.

---
title: Fable: The Economy
tags: economy, money, wealth, ledger, currency, properties, treasury, jobs, lifestyle, starving, net worth, buy, invest, prosperity
---

Money is THREE pools, never one stat: pocket WEALTH (fully liquid), property TREASURIES (per business/estate/settlement, owner-gated), and pack items (fiction; v1 has no shops or item pricing). Net worth is derived at render only. The currency label is learned in play (the ledger's currency verb; no hardcoded "gold").

PROPERTIES (authored on cards or founded in play, cap 8) accrue revenue minus upkeep daily; seven consecutive deficit days and creditors seize. Jobs (cap 2) pay wages without the NPC present, but lapse after three absent days. Lifestyle (Squatter free, then Modest, Comfortable, Aristocratic) charges daily at the current node's prosperity; insolvency stamps Starving (a real debuff with a stamina drain) until solvency returns. Buying is a sink: the coin leaves the books. Node prosperity (hard times through booming) scales revenue and prices. The [LEDGER] bracket is the money verb (found, deposit, withdraw, invest, buy, lifestyle, job, wealth); the World tab's Ledger section and the Player vitals render it all.

---
title: Fable: NPCs and Relationships
tags: npc, cast, registry, presence, mood, intent, relationships, bonds, promises, reaper, dossier, party, export, import
---

NPCs run a prominence axis: CORE (the authored cast, never archived), NAMED (discovered in play, tracked), and ambient extras (untracked flavor). The tracked interior holds mood, a distilled intent, the worn outfit, held items, and relationship milestones. Relationships are tier ladders (from Stranger up through Trusted and Bonded, down through Rival and Nemesis) that modulate temperature, never voice.

On-camera presence is asserted per turn and ages out after a few turns' absence; the world block renders present NPCs' minds, wardrobe, and held items. Promises owed to present givers accrue frustration on a volatility curve. Long-absent named NPCs archive to stubs unless protected by a bond extreme, an open task, or carried items.

The Party panel's cast cards open the NPC dossier (identity, bond, dispatched tasks, interior, last seen vs on-camera). A played NPC can be exported as a shareable card; an npc card can be imported off-screen for the narrator to introduce organically.

---
title: Fable: Quests, Rumors, and Threads
tags: quests, objectives, deadlines, rumors, threats, threads, milestones, clocks, auto fail, promises
---

QUESTS: a short active list (cap 8) with objectives, optional cur/total progress, and optional deadlines. The World tab renders each quest as a conic progress clock; overdue flips red; overdue quests not given by the player auto-fail on the tick. Done and fail close and remove. No difficulty numbers exist on any quest shape, by mandate.

RUMORS: heard at places, stored capped, propagated by the world tick; threat-flavored rumors seed Suspected dangers at that place's map entrance. MILESTONES: relationship beats recorded on the NPC's record. THREADS: every terminal kill or live removal opens a causal thread on that site; a later event, re-entry, or time closes it. The narrator feels threads only through the re-entry digest.

---
title: Fable: Editing the Story
tags: edit, delete, reroll, swipe, variants, rewind, slice regenerate, golden pencil, rollback, intro variants, toolrail
---

Hover a beat for its glass toolrail (latest two beats only). EDIT rewrites prose in place; the trailing beat re-tracks world state against the new text, deeper edits need a rewind first. DELETE removes downward: the trailing beat goes outright, deeper deletes open a count-first confirm.

Variants: every narrator beat keeps its swipes and rerolls. The ‹ › bar (AI beats) and ▲ ▼ capsule (user beats) step them; stepping past the last variant folds into a reroll; ‹ or › mid-reroll interrupts and restarts. Each variant is a COHERENT TIMELINE: a reroll reverts the world to the turn's start, and installing an old swipe installs the world it produced.

The golden pencil: select text inside an AI beat, click the brass pencil at the selection's edge, and the API regenerates ONLY that span in place (prose polish; no state change, no undo). The opening beat edits write through to the card itself; new intro variants are created by rerolling it.

---
title: Ghost Writer and Crossroads
tags: ghost writer, crossroads, composer, aids, swipe, continue, impersonate, options, dc, decks, fork
---

Two composer aids sit directly right of the input; both need the API and go inert (with a notice chip, never silent) while a turn runs.

GHOST WRITER: Swipe rerolls the beat with a steering nudge; Continue extends the last beat (state re-tracked); Impersonate drafts the PLAYER'S next line into the composer for review, never auto-sent.

CROSSROADS: draw six story forks from five decks (Player, World with established people only, NPC which introduces someone new, Plot, Explicit). Expand drops the fully written fork into the composer; nothing auto-sends. The grounding law: options spend only what the world state shows the player holding, obstacles stay map-real, and a skilled act declares its check inline, like "[Lockpicking DC 18]"; the game honors that pinned DC verbatim when the player acts.

---
title: Fable: The Tab Rail and Raw Editors
tags: tab rail, tabs, player tab, world tab, npc tab, raw editor, json, xml, edit, vitals, quests, ledger
---

The Wupi drawer's tab rail: Player · Sim Card · Codex · World · NPC; single-active, resets on drawer close, all caps display. PLAYER vitals: the Health tier, Stamina, Wealth (pocket coin), Net Worth when it differs from pocket, Active Effects with polarity coloring, equipment across all nine slots. SIM CARD relabels itself by subtype (NPC/World/Scenario Card); npc cards show a Wealth row (their properties' treasuries; hidden while the economy is dormant). WORLD: Date (click-to-edit inline), Time (12-hour), Weather, Tone, Location, Quests as progress clocks (overdue red), and a Ledger section when the economy is live. NPC: the tracked cast.

Every tab carries a pencil raw editor: a centered modal over that tab's own data (the world/player/npc JSON split files, or the card XML) with live validation (save stays disabled while invalid), atomic writes, and revert. The Player editor combines the saved player's XML and the live player.json under a divider; it hides while no player is bound.

---
title: Playground: The Sand Table
tags: playground, sand table, god mode, wand, testing, auto-pass, freeze-clamps, cheat, debug, experiments
---

The Playground (the Sand Table) is god mode over the live simulation: a testing table for whoever runs the game, not a player menu. Reveal it with the WAND icon in the Wupi drawer's brand header (right drawer); the [PLAYGROUND ACTIVE] strip slides over the tab rail with three scrollable panels: PLAYER / NPC / WORLD. Results render as inline report lines; guard errors surface verbatim.

PLAYER: the god flags AUTO-PASS (every skill check succeeds, combat injuries skipped, rest never interrupted) and FREEZE CLAMPS (fatigue and starvation floors off; the underlying math still runs and reports), wealth deltas, item tags, hazard rolls in exact live-referee parity, forced rest, interruption, and tick loops (up to 8 passes, one combined diff report).

NPC: relationship set, interior set, REVIVE (resurrect the archived or terminal; god-mode only), registry alias/rename/remove (Named only; the Core cast is pinned), milestone injection.

WORLD: time skip (bypasses pacing clamps), force tick, asset spawn/remove (carried loot drops), site threat set, history restore.

Flags are session-scoped, never persisted. Every mutator snapshots into the undo ring + autosaves first, so Playground actions undo through the same history as everything else.

---
title: The Stage and the Feed
tags: stage, feed, beats, portraits, typing indicator, transitions, burn, particles, uppercase, messages, streaming
---

Fable's stage is full-screen immersion with no settings menus. Beats render as rounded glass cards with flush portraits dissolving into the inner margin (assistant left, user mirrored right); no portrait collapses the avatar column. The feed pages 50 messages at a time (scroll up to prepend more); narrator prose is selectable (the golden pencil builds on that). Streaming arrives live under a brass caret; the typing indicator reads "(Name) is currently typing..." on npc cards, "Narrator is currently thinking..." otherwise.

Scene dressing: stage backgrounds behind glass readability panels, burn transitions on scene shifts, weather particle FX, and the splash-wipe entry on boot. All text in BOTH drawers renders uppercase for display only; the underlying text is untouched.

---
title: Stage Backgrounds and Scene Art
tags: backgrounds, background gallery, scene art, images, cropper, prism, wallpapers, stage
---

Stage backgrounds are a GLOBAL image library at apps/fable/images/backgrounds/ (flat PNG/JPG), managed from the Wupi drawer's background-gallery foot button: import, crop per-card in the background cropper, select. The active background is save-persistent per card and the AI never touches it. The stage paints it contain-fit behind glass panels that keep the text readable.

Generated scene art inside Fable does NOT exist, by design: image generation is PRISM-only and deliberately unhooked from the narrator; art requests route to PRISM, not into the story.

---
title: PRISM
tags: prism, images, image generation, gallery, stable diffusion, art, glass vault, fork, hires, noobai, composer, slots
---

PRISM is the image-generation studio: fully local Stable Diffusion (the NoobAI-XL checkpoint) on the machine's GPU, offline at runtime, nothing leaves the folder. Launched from the home grid; while PRISM renders, the chat model swaps out of VRAM and returns afterward.

COMPOSER: three quick-pick chip slots (Subject & Count and Framing & Pose mandatory, Environment recommended), then a freeform Danbooru-tag lane (search the catalog, click to add pills; typed text never becomes a pill). CLIP reads prompts sequentially, so the compiled prompt is always subject, framing, pose, environment, freeform regardless of click order. Knobs: bucket size presets (default 832x1216), the NSFW and Furry steering toggles, and a random seed.

THE GLASS VAULT: the masonry gallery with favorites, prompt search, Send to Composer, Fork & Edit (a seed-locked A/B split screen with a drag-compare slider), and trash with restore; purge is permanent. Every render is two-stage by locked recipe: a base pass plus a mandatory hires refine (1.5x) over the RealESRGAN upscaler scaffold; the quality prefix, negative block, and subject-tag gate (solo / no humans) all auto-apply invisibly.

---
title: Updates and Data Preservation
tags: updates, updater, portable, preserve, versions, wupi.zip, clean break, release
---

WUPI ships as a portable WUPI.zip with no installer and nothing to configure. Updating: the update panel (paw menu) downloads the new zip, stages it in temp, exits, and a separate updater.exe copies it in and relaunches, because a running exe cannot overwrite itself. The preserve rule is absolute: user data (memory/, models/, apps/ with cards, players, saves, the codex library, and the PRISM gallery, plus personal data/ files) is never touched. Engine content (wupi.sim, wupi.codex, wupi.prompt, fable.prompt) is replaced verbatim on every update; hand edits to those four files are lost by design.

v0.30.0 is a CLEAN BREAK: pre-v2 cards are invisible to every load and edit path, and pre-0.30.0-era saves of v2 cards may not load. Re-create old cards through the Creator.

---
title: Logs and Crash Reports
tags: logs, crash, troubleshooting, diagnostics, sd abort, panic, support
---

For troubleshooting, WUPI writes: session logs at logs/wupi-<date>-<time>.log (logging starts at the home reveal; newest 10 kept, line-flushed); crash reports at logs/crash-<date>-<time>.log (the panic plus a recent-activity tail; newest 10 kept) with the full backtrace at %TEMP%\wupi_panic.txt; and PRISM Stable-Diffusion aborts at logs/sd-abort.txt. Read that plus the [sd] engine lines before touching anything when a render fails.

The logs/ folder is user territory: the pruners only touch WUPI's own file shapes. Boot runs silent by design: the loading screen, the updater gate, and the model load all happen before logging starts.